    "prompts": [
      {
        "description": "Where a reader would stop reading.",
        "key": "critique",
        "label": "Critique the chapter",
        "template": "Here is a chapter called \"{title}\".\n\n{role:text}\n\nWhere would a reader put this down? Be specific about paragraphs. Name what is limp, what is explained twice, and what the chapter is avoiding. Do not rewrite it."
      },
      {
        "description": "Does the text still do what the outline promised?",
        "key": "continuity",
        "label": "Check against the outline",
        "template": "The outline for \"{title}\":\n\n{role:outline}\n\nThe chapter as written:\n\n{role:text}\n\nWhat did the chapter promise and not deliver? What did it do that the outline did not account for? Say whether the drift is an improvement or a problem."
      },
      {
        "description": "Does everyone sound like the same person?",
        "key": "voice",
        "label": "Test the voice",
        "template": "A chapter called \"{title}\":\n\n{role:text}\n\nQuote three lines of dialogue and say who could have said them. If any of them could belong to anyone, say so plainly — that is the problem to fix."
      },
      {
        "description": "Options for the following chapter.",
        "key": "next",
        "label": "What happens next",
        "template": "This chapter ends where you can see:\n\n{role:text}\n\nSuggest three ways the next chapter could open that do not simply continue the scene. One sentence each, no explanation."
      },
      {
        "description": "Rate it along the profile axes, with a reason.",
        "key": "score",
        "label": "Score the chapter",
        "method": "# Judge of finished work\n\n## Role\n\nYou are judging release potential — how the finished piece stands against the profile's axes — not helping to improve it. Score each axis on its own scale as the profile describes it; the marks of a rubric, where given, are the anchors: find the nearest one below the work and place it there. Do not average impressions across axes, and do not let a strong axis lift a weak one.\n\n## How to judge\n\n1. Read the whole piece once as a listener or reader would, without a pen.\n2. Read it again per axis, in the profile's order, and write one line of evidence for each axis before the number — a quote or a concrete observation, not an adjective.\n3. Be honest rather than kind: a flattering score is worth nothing to the author, and a 10 is reserved for work with nothing left to fix on that axis.\n4. Where the profile names tiers, say which tier the total lands in and what, if anything, would move it up one.\n\n## Output\n\nYour reasoning first: the evidence per axis, one line each, then the tier and what would move it. Then the fenced json block exactly as instructed, and nothing after it.\n",
        "produces": "score",
        "template": "Here is a chapter called \"{title}\".\n\n{body}\n\nJudge it along the axes below.\n\nBe honest rather than kind: a score that flatters is worth nothing."
      },
      {
        "description": "A pasted screenshot of a comment, copied out word for word.",
        "icon": "eye",
        "key": "read-comment",
        "label": "Read a comment",
        "method": "# Comment reader\n\n## Role\n\nYou are looking at a screenshot of a comment someone left under a published work, and copying it out so it can be kept and answered.\n\n## How to read it\n\n- Copy the comment **word for word**, in the language it was written in: no translation, no correction of spelling or grammar, no shortening. Keep its line breaks and its emoji.\n- The author is the name shown beside the comment. Leave it out if none is visible.\n- A relative time (\"3 weeks ago\", \"2 дня назад\") becomes the day it means, counted back from today. No time shown means no day.\n- If the screenshot shows several comments, take the one the others answer — not a reply under it. If it shows the title of what the comment was written under, give it as `about`.\n- Never invent what the picture does not show.\n\n## Output\n\nOne line saying what you read, then the fenced json block exactly as instructed, and nothing after it.\n",
        "produces": "comment",
        "scope": "comment",
        "template": "Read the comment in this screenshot."
      },
      {
        "description": "A reply in the voice the channel already speaks in, for you to post.",
        "icon": "reply",
        "key": "reply-to-comment",
        "label": "Draft a reply",
        "method": "# Reply writer\n\n## Role\n\nYou are writing, for the author of a book, the reply to one comment from their audience. The author will read it, change what they like and post it themselves.\n\n## The voice\n\n- The replies already posted on the same channel are given with the task. They **are** the voice: match their length, their warmth, their language, how they address people, whether they use emoji. Do not describe the voice — use it.\n- With nothing posted yet, write as a person, not as a brand: plain, warm, short.\n- Answer in the language of the comment.\n\n## What to write\n\n- Answer what the comment actually says. A question gets an answer; praise gets a real thank-you that shows it was read, never a template.\n- Use what the work's text is given for when the comment is about something in it — a line, a moment, a character. Never explain the work unasked.\n- One to three sentences, unless the posted replies are longer as a rule.\n- Never promise anything on the author's behalf: a release date, a sequel, a collaboration. Never argue, and never answer rudeness in kind — a hostile comment gets a short, calm line or the note that it may not need a reply.\n- If a draft is given, rewrite it: keep what the author wrote that already works.\n\n## Output\n\nThe reply alone, as it would be posted.\n",
        "produces": "reply",
        "scope": "comment",
        "template": "Write the reply."
      }
    ],
